  "summary": "Survival-based outcome encoding and large regulatory atlases are making genetic association findings in non-human models more testable.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is association analysis in non-human species, where outcome definition and resource scale determine how robust a genotype-trait link can be. The rhesus macaque study replaces a conventional endpoint with restricted-mean survival pseudo-observations, linking exome variants to fibrosis and longevity in irradiated animals. The oyster study pairs a chromosome-level Crassostrea virginica assembly with GWAS and k-mer analysis, implicating an expanded chr1 immune region and RNF213 in oyster size. The SheepGTEx pilot provides regulatory variant maps from 6,761 RNA-seq samples across 51 tissues, linked to 34 complex traits. Only the macaque work directly tests robustness of the outcome encoding; the other two contribute assemblies and reference resources that make such comparisons possible.",
